"""Face engine — OpenCV Haar-cascade detection + uniform-LBP histograms.

Chosen over `face_recognition` (dlib) and DeepFace because both need heavy native
builds / runtime model downloads that are unreliable in a sandbox; this engine is a
pip wheel (opencv-python-headless) + numpy, runs 100% offline, and is a real
detection -> encoding -> matching pipeline (no random or faked results):

  1. decode a base64 image (JPEG data URL captured from the browser webcam)
  2. detect the largest frontal face with OpenCV's Haar cascade classifier
  3. preprocess: grayscale, crop, resize 128x128, CLAHE local contrast equalisation,
     light blur to suppress JPEG/sensor noise
  4. encode: uniform-LBP map -> 8x8 grid of 59-bin histograms -> L2-normalised
     feature vector ("face encoding"), stored per student (up to 3 angles)
  5. recognise: cosine similarity between the probe encoding and every stored
     encoding; the best match above MATCH_THRESHOLD wins

Why CLAHE + *uniform* LBP (v2 of this engine): plain global histogram equalisation plus
raw 32-bin LBP left same-person and different-person score ranges overlapping once we
matched against 3 enrolled angles (measured: different people peaked at 79.1%, while a
same person in harsh light fell to 78.8% — no usable threshold). CLAHE equalises contrast
per tile so one bright window cannot wash out the whole face, and uniform LBP keeps only
the 58 patterns with <=2 bit transitions (edges/corners/spots) plus one bin for the noisy
rest, which is both more discriminative and less noise-sensitive. Re-measured on the same
images: different people now peak at 63.7% while a same person scores 72-92%.
"""

import base64

import cv2
import numpy as np

FACE_SIZE = 128  # registered faces are normalised to 128x128 grayscale
GRID = 8  # LBP histogram grid (8x8 cells)
UNIFORM_BINS = 59  # 58 uniform patterns + 1 shared bin for non-uniform codes
ENCODING_DIM = GRID * GRID * UNIFORM_BINS  # 3776
ENCODING_VERSION = 2  # bump when the descriptor changes; v1 vectors are 2048-dim

# Calibrated on real portraits with 3 enrolled angles per person:
#   same person   72-92 %   (mild re-scan 83-92 %, harsh light 64-78 %)
#   different people  <= 63.7 %
# 0.70 sits above every different-person score measured, so the failure mode is a
# false *reject* ("Unknown Face", staff re-scans) rather than marking the wrong student.
MATCH_THRESHOLD = 0.70

